00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:03.710
Nicole: As we have already discussed
written communications can take

00:00:03.730 --> 00:00:08.010
different forms and may involve
reports, policy documents even

00:00:08.059 --> 00:00:10.160
emails and instruction manuals.

00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:15.510
The way written communications are
put together and the tone used to

00:00:15.510 --> 00:00:20.180
impart the information, are important
and should be set at a level that the

00:00:20.190 --> 00:00:23.840
different audiences reading them can
understand for them to be effective.

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:28.180
Let's look at some of the elements that
make written communications effective.

00:00:30.059 --> 00:00:33.580
Abraham: Written communication should have
a clear purpose and should not be vague.

00:00:34.029 --> 00:00:37.150
The purpose should be clearly stated
at the beginning of the communication

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:39.300
defining what it wants to achieve.

00:00:39.730 --> 00:00:42.150
The reader knows what they are reading
about straight away about without

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:43.660
having to wade through ramblings.

00:00:44.139 --> 00:00:47.210
A good example of this would be
documenting a threat and clearly

00:00:47.240 --> 00:00:50.260
putting together a report on
any imminent threat attacks.

00:00:51.450 --> 00:00:54.660
Nicole: Not only should it be
clear about the purpose the message

00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:57.840
behind the written communication
should be clear what the goal is.

00:00:58.230 --> 00:01:02.460
An example of a clear message would be
to ensure that a team understands what

00:01:02.470 --> 00:01:07.790
is expected and can align themselves with
that goal such as being aware of any cyber

00:01:07.790 --> 00:01:12.800
security protocols related to current
activities or future threat actors.

00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:16.679
Abraham: It is important that
written communication is accurate

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:19.830
so that people reading it can have
confidence on any data displayed.

00:01:20.490 --> 00:01:23.730
This is really important when
creating manuals or giving directions

00:01:23.730 --> 00:01:25.110
to other members of a team.

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:29.430
Nicole: Attention to detail means
that all the information needed is

00:01:29.430 --> 00:01:33.399
contained in the written communication
and the reader doesn't have to look

00:01:33.410 --> 00:01:35.400
elsewhere for further clarification.

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:40.900
This can also help give clear guidance
that’s easy to follow in manuals and

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:44.610
instruction booklets prevent mistakes
being made or misunderstandings.

00:01:45.469 --> 00:01:48.769
Abraham: The correct use of spelling,
grammar and punctuation are very

00:01:48.859 --> 00:01:51.940
important as errors can change
the meaning of a message which

00:01:51.940 --> 00:01:53.510
might result in misinformation.

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:58.420
Correct usage also gives the reader more
confidence in the communication and more

00:01:58.420 --> 00:02:00.619
likely to act on the information imparted.

00:02:01.630 --> 00:02:04.109
Nicole: The 'tone of voice'
of the written communication

00:02:04.299 --> 00:02:07.580
should be right for the audience
concerned, so that it communicates

00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:09.470
how it should be responded to.

00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:13.760
If the tone is too relaxed it won’t
communicate any urgency that maybe

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:15.319
involved in the communication.

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:19.160
Abraham: The written communication
should have an appropriate layout

00:02:19.170 --> 00:02:21.869
which makes it clearer to read
and understand the key points.

00:02:22.369 --> 00:02:24.969
It can also make it easier to
find the appropriate sections

00:02:24.969 --> 00:02:26.129
within the communication.

00:02:26.509 --> 00:02:29.169
This is especially important
when putting reports together

00:02:29.179 --> 00:02:33.670
Nicole: So, we can see how the skill
of written communication is really

00:02:33.670 --> 00:02:37.503
important as cyber security professionals
need to make sure that they are

00:02:37.503 --> 00:02:39.229
on the same page with their team.

00:02:40.010 --> 00:02:43.930
They also often need to communicate
with everyone else in a company or

00:02:43.930 --> 00:02:48.630
organisation and are responsible for
sharing important information with

00:02:48.630 --> 00:02:53.070
people who aren’t well versed in the
technical jargon and language of cyber

00:02:53.070 --> 00:02:58.379
security, so being mindful about how they
communicate can make all the difference.

